# .mavis-private.example — copy to `.mavis-private` (which is gitignored) and edit.
#
# The leak guard (`node scripts/check-leaks.mjs`) derives most of what counts as "personal"
# straight from your own gitignored brain: every directory under `projects/`, the `name:` in
# `identity/profile.md`, your `git config user.name` and `user.email`, your OS username and home
# directory. Run `node scripts/check-leaks.mjs --explain` to see the full derived list and where
# each term came from.
#
# This file covers the three things derivation cannot get right on its own.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# deny: <term>
#   Something personal the brain does not know about.
#
#   Use it for a client you have never made a project directory for, a live promo code, an
#   internal hostname, or a project slug shorter than 4 characters (the derivation skips those
#   deliberately, because short terms fire inside unrelated words).
#
#   An explicit deny has no minimum length — a 3-letter client code is honoured.

# deny: acme-corp
# deny: LAUNCH25
# deny: internal.example.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# allow: <term>
#   A derived term that is not actually a secret, silenced everywhere.
#
#   Two common cases. First, a project slug that is also an ordinary word — if you have a project
#   directory called `team`, `core` or `notes`, the guard would fire on any sentence containing
#   it. Second, an identity you have deliberately chosen to publish: if your name is the copyright
#   holder in LICENSE, it is public by design and should not block every commit.
#
#   Allow is a blunt instrument — it silences the term in every file. Prefer `allowpath` when only
#   one file legitimately needs it.

# allow: team
# allow: YourName

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# allowpath: <path>
#   A whole file that is allowed to contain derived terms.
#
#   Some files legitimately must: LICENSE names the copyright holder, and .gitignore describes the
#   very directories the terms are derived from. Without this they would fire on every commit
#   forever, which is exactly how a guard teaches its owner to type --no-verify by reflex.
#
#   Matching is simple: exact path, a directory prefix ending in `/`, or a trailing `*`.

# allowpath: LICENSE
# allowpath: .gitignore
# allowpath: docs/examples/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Per-line escape
#   For a single line that genuinely needs a term, put `leak-guard-allow` in a comment on that
#   line. It stays visible in review, unlike a file-wide exemption.
#
# One-off override
#   git commit --no-verify
